  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nCurrently, a near 5,940m ridge is centered to our west with high \npressure in place across the eastern U.S. The anomalous ridge and \nlingering moisture will continue the current heat wave. A slow \neastward shift of the ridge Wednesday and Thursday will push \ntemperatures even further into the 90s, potentially near 100 degrees \nfor parts of the southern Valley. Heat index values will reach or \nexceed 100 for most places with 105 possible in some southern areas \nWednesday or Thursday. Little relief from clouds or diurnal \nconvection can be expected because of strong subsidence and limited \ncoverage outside of the mountains. For reference, here are the \nrecord highs through the next 3 days. \n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge     \n09-02   100(2011)      98(2011)       96(1943)       99(1953)       \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n\nBy Friday, the pattern begins to shift slightly as an upper low \ntracks into the northeastern U.S., leading to suppression of the \nridge and more northwesterly flow aloft. This will decrease \ntemperatures and lead to a slight uptick in rain chances, \npotentially outside of the mountains. Moisture will increase by \nSaturday, along with a frontal boundary drifting down from the \nnortheast, further increasing chances for showers and storms. The \nfront will remain near or just to our east as troughing digs through \nLabor Day. This will keep a more eastward focus on isolated showers \nand storms, but most places will likely stay dry. Temperatures will \nbe moderated lower in the 90s but still above normal.",
